  "text": "[calm] Everything that synchronizes here obeys one clock: the narration's own playback time. [short pause] The voice comes first — generated on this laptop from a written script. Back comes the audio, plus a sidecar file listing every word's start and end, to the millisecond. You are looking at that file now. These captions are built from it, and lit by it. [short pause] A cue that lands on a word takes that word's time — copied, not guessed. If a room should change where I say \"docks,\" the cue's time is that word's time. [pause] Every sync bug in these films was the same bug: something kept its own time. [short pause] The worst one came at the trailer's turning point. Two choices on a screen — light mode, dark mode — and a third appears: build whatever you want. A cursor drifts up to choose it. That cursor ran on the CSS animation clock. Under load, that clock fell behind — and the scene cut away before the cursor could arrive. The film's most profound moment, ruined by a second clock nobody was watching. [short pause] It keeps the voice's time now. That became the rule: if it moves, it takes the voice's clock. [pause] One more scar. You cannot pause a voice mid-track without clipping a syllable. When a film needs true silence, the voice is rendered as separate tracks, with real silence between. The trailer's narration is eight files for exactly this reason. [short pause] And the music. The score playing under this film right now was computed from this film's own cue sheet. Move a cue, re-render — and it lands on the new time, bit for bit, every run. [short pause] In fact — here comes a crescendo.",
